4.14.649.070 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"answered\",\n  \"answer\": \"Double circulation is a process in which blood passes through the heart twice during one complete cycle. In humans, this system is important because it ensures that oxygenated and deoxygenated blood are efficiently separated and transported to different parts of the body. The process involves two distinct circulations: pulmonary circulation and systemic circulation. In pulmonary circulation, deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle is pumped to the lungs via the pulmonary artery, where it gets oxygenated. The oxygenated blood then returns to the left atrium via the pulmonary veins. In systemic circulation, oxygenated blood from the left ventricle is pumped through the aorta to the rest of the body, delivering oxygen and nutrients to tissues. The deoxygenated blood then returns to the right atrium via veins. This double circulation ensures that the body receives a continuous supply of oxygen and nutrients while efficiently removing waste products.\",\n  \"selected_evidence_ids\": [\"E1\", \"E3\"],\n  \"citations\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E1\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 66,\n      \"textbook_page\": 57\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E3\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 72,\n      \"textbook_page\": 63\n    }\n  ],\n  \"missing_information\": []\n}"}
