6.19.444.329 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"answered\",\n  \"answer\": \"Mitosis and meiosis are two types of cell division, but they have several key differences. Mitosis occurs in all body cells and results in two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell. It is used for growth, repair, and asexual reproduction. Meiosis, on the other hand, occurs only in sex cells (gametes) and results in four daughter cells that are genetically different from the parent cell. It is used for sexual reproduction and reduces the chromosome number by half. Mitosis involves one division, while meiosis involves two divisions. Additionally, meiosis includes a process called crossing over, where genetic material is exchanged between homologous chromosomes, increasing genetic diversity. In contrast, mitosis does not involve crossing over. The main purpose of mitosis is to maintain the same number of chromosomes in the daughter cells, while the main purpose of meiosis is to produce gametes with half the number of chromosomes.\",\n  \"selected_evidence_ids\": [\"E1\", \"E2\"],\n  \"citations\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E1\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 150,\n      \"textbook_page\": 141\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E2\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 151,\n      \"textbook_page\": 142\n    }\n  ],\n  \"missing_information\": []\n}"}
