11.03.700.827 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"answered\",\n  \"answer\": \"To find the distance of the image formed by a concave mirror, we use the mirror formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u. First, we need to determine the focal length (f) of the mirror. The radius of curvature (R) is given as 8 cm, and for a concave mirror, the focal length is half of the radius of curvature. Therefore, f = R/2 = 8 cm / 2 = 4 cm. The object distance (u) is given as -10 cm (negative because the object is placed in front of the mirror). Substituting these values into the mirror formula: 1/4 = 1/v + 1/(-10). Solving for 1/v gives 1/v = 1/4 - 1/10 = 5/20 - 2/20 = 3/20. Therefore, v = 20/3 ≈ 6.67 cm. The image is formed at a distance of approximately 6.67 cm from the pole of the mirror on the same side as the object.\",\n  \"selected_evidence_ids\": [\"E1\", \"E2\", \"E3\"],\n  \"citations\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E1\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 13,\n      \"textbook_page\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E2\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 26,\n      \"textbook_page\": 16\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E3\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 29,\n      \"textbook_page\": 19\n    }\n  ],\n  \"missing_information\": []\n}"}
